// EXPECT-FAIL: typecheck
// ✅ NOW A TYPECHECK ERROR WITH A SOURCE SPAN (F1-3b / F1 §3.1 N2, 2026-07-18): the
//    ambiguity was detected in codegen's func_map suffix SCAN and reported span-less;
//    it now fires in the type_checker (file:line:col), which is what let the scan be
//    deleted. The checker already knew `contains` was ambiguous (two decls share the
//    bare name); the locality rule (a file that DEFINES the name resolves it locally,
//    like `contains` inside string.nova) keeps this from over-firing.
//    (History: was `codegen`, and before that `compiler-crash` — a Zig backtrace.)
// NEGATIVE test: must FAIL to compile (design/F1 §3.1 N2).
//
// A bare `contains(...)` names two functions — `string.contains` and `assert.contains`
// — and the caller's file defines neither, so which one is meant is undecidable.
//
// N2: **ambiguity is a compile error, never a silent pick.**
//
// Why this is worth a case even though nothing was miscompiling: F1 stage 2
// measured the scan across four real programs — reached up to 49 times, ambiguous
// ZERO times. So the scan was a landmine that had not gone off. It detonates the
// day someone adds a colliding name, and the failure lands nowhere near the edit,
// because the pick is not random — it is arbitrary and STABLE, and flips only when
// unrelated code changes the insertion order or grows the map. This turns that
// landmine into a tripwire: it rejects nothing that compiled before (corpus green
// when it landed) and refuses to guess from now on.
import assert;
import string;

@test
fn t_ambiguous_bare_call(): void {
    // `string.contains(...)` or `assert.contains(...)` would both be fine.
    // Unqualified, the compiler must refuse to choose.
    let ok = contains("haystack", "hay");
    assert.isTrue(ok);
}
